1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,732
Mexico, the year six eighty-three.

2
00:00:02,732 --> 00:00:09,295
A king is lowered into the heart of a pyramid, and a great stone slab is carved to cover him.

3
00:00:09,295 --> 00:00:14,800
Thirteen centuries later, a book says that slab shows a man piloting a rocket.

4
00:00:15,266 --> 00:00:17,091
The picture is everywhere.

5
00:00:17,091 --> 00:00:19,969
Documentaries, T-shirts, a million posts.

6
00:00:19,969 --> 00:00:23,688
A man leaning back, hands forward, flames behind him.

7
00:00:23,688 --> 00:00:26,145
It really does look like a cockpit.

8
00:00:26,145 --> 00:00:27,899
Until you turn it around.

9
00:00:28,366 --> 00:00:32,780
Tonight we go down the same hidden stair the archaeologist took.

10
00:00:32,780 --> 00:00:35,055
Seven levels, to the tomb itself.

11
00:00:35,055 --> 00:00:39,399
And at every level, we test the astronaut against the evidence.

12
00:00:39,866 --> 00:00:42,024
Is that a pilot at the controls?

13
00:00:42,024 --> 00:00:44,720
Or a king at the foot of the world tree?

14
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:48,833
And is there anything in this tomb that is truly unexplained?

15
00:00:48,833 --> 00:00:50,114
There is one thing.

16
00:00:50,114 --> 00:00:51,800
It is not what you think.

17
00:00:52,266 --> 00:00:55,033
The descent starts at the very top, with a book.

18
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:01,108
Nineteen sixty-eight.

19
00:01:01,108 --> 00:01:05,628
A Swiss hotel manager, Erich von Daniken, publishes a book.

20
00:01:05,628 --> 00:01:08,923
In English it becomes Chariots of the Gods.

21
00:01:08,923 --> 00:01:13,366
By nineteen eighty-seven it has sold seven million copies.

22
00:01:13,833 --> 00:01:22,400
Archaeologists noted, with some despair, that he had sold more books about archaeology than any archaeologist who ever lived.

23
00:01:22,866 --> 00:01:25,141
In it, a drawing from Palenque.

24
00:01:25,141 --> 00:01:35,266
He compares the reclining king to an American Mercury astronaut, reads the tree as a rocket, and the jaws at the bottom as exhaust flames.

25
00:01:35,733 --> 00:01:38,853
And the drawing is printed turned on its side.

26
00:01:38,853 --> 00:01:42,312
Turned that way, it does look like a man in a seat.

27
00:01:42,312 --> 00:01:43,872
Hold on to that detail.

28
00:01:43,872 --> 00:01:45,500
We will come back to it.

29
00:01:45,966 --> 00:01:47,650
Even the caption slips.

30
00:01:47,650 --> 00:01:51,018
It says the relief is from Copan, in Honduras.

31
00:01:51,018 --> 00:01:53,141
The text gets Palenque right.

32
00:01:53,141 --> 00:01:57,900
A small error, but it tells you how closely the page was checked.

33
00:01:58,366 --> 00:02:00,596
And the idea was not even new.

34
00:02:00,596 --> 00:02:09,146
Researchers at Bad Archaeology trace the description to an earlier article by two French writers, Guy Tarade and A.

35
00:02:09,146 --> 00:02:09,666
Millou.

36
00:02:10,133 --> 00:02:16,995
The claim, then, is an ancient astronaut, worshipped by the Maya, and buried under his own portrait.

37
00:02:16,995 --> 00:02:17,818
Easy to say.

38
00:02:17,818 --> 00:02:24,199
To test it, we need the real object, and the real object was hidden for twelve hundred years.

39
00:02:24,666 --> 00:02:27,353
Palenque, in the rainforest of Chiapas.

40
00:02:27,353 --> 00:02:29,488
The Temple of the Inscriptions.

41
00:02:29,488 --> 00:02:37,066
For a century and more it looked like what it appears to be, a pyramid with a temple on top, and nothing more.

42
00:02:37,533 --> 00:02:46,086
In the late nineteen forties, the Mexican archaeologist Alberto Ruz Lhuillier notices something odd in the temple floor.

43
00:02:46,086 --> 00:02:50,933
One slab has holes in it, and the holes are filled with stone plugs.

44
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:53,235
Floors do not have handles.

45
00:02:53,235 --> 00:02:54,460
He lifts the slab.

46
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:59,900
Beneath it, a stairway, packed solid with rubble, leading down into the pyramid.

47
00:03:00,366 --> 00:03:02,585
It takes four seasons to empty.

48
00:03:02,585 --> 00:03:04,803
Hot, damp, narrow, in the dark.

49
00:03:04,803 --> 00:03:12,533
Basket by basket, the stair goes down through the body of the pyramid, below the level of the plaza outside.

50
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,646
At the bottom, in the rubble, the remains of sacrificed attendants.

51
00:03:17,646 --> 00:03:21,599
And behind them, a triangular stone slab, sealing a wall.

52
00:03:22,066 --> 00:03:24,653
June fifteenth, nineteen fifty-two.

53
00:03:24,653 --> 00:03:25,983
The slab is moved.

54
00:03:25,983 --> 00:03:31,599
Ruz steps into a vault that no human being had entered for twelve centuries.

55
00:03:32,066 --> 00:03:36,771
The walls are lined with stucco figures, nine of them, standing guard.

56
00:03:36,771 --> 00:03:40,199
And in the middle of the room, a vast carved stone.

57
00:03:40,666 --> 00:03:43,567
At the centre, a monolithic sarcophagus.

58
00:03:43,567 --> 00:03:51,400
Over it, a single slab of limestone, about three and a half metres long, carved on its top and on its sides.

59
00:03:51,866 --> 00:03:55,352
The slab is far too big to have come down the stairs.

60
00:03:55,352 --> 00:03:59,365
The tomb was sealed first, and the pyramid was built over it.

61
00:03:59,365 --> 00:04:01,733
This was made to stay shut for ever.

62
00:04:02,199 --> 00:04:03,763
Ruz waits for months.

63
00:04:03,763 --> 00:04:13,066
Then, in November, his team raises the five-ton slab a few centimetres at a time, with logs and jacks under the four corners.

64
00:04:13,533 --> 00:04:17,348
Underneath, a second smaller lid, held by stone plugs.

65
00:04:17,348 --> 00:04:23,566
And under that, the king, stained red with cinnabar, wearing a mask and strings of jade.

66
00:04:24,033 --> 00:04:30,799
The mask alone is built from hundreds of pieces of jade, with eyes of shell and obsidian.

67
00:04:30,799 --> 00:04:34,600
To the Maya, jade meant breath, life, and royalty.

68
00:04:35,066 --> 00:04:41,200
From the sarcophagus, a hollow stone tube rises along the stair to the temple floor.

69
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:43,829
Archaeologists call it a psychoduct.

70
00:04:43,829 --> 00:04:49,233
The likely purpose: to keep the dead king in contact with the world above.

71
00:04:49,699 --> 00:04:51,409
Look at what this means.

72
00:04:51,409 --> 00:04:55,898
A stair going down, a tube coming up, a lid carved with a road.

73
00:04:55,898 --> 00:05:00,600
If this is a machine, it is a machine for crossing between worlds.

74
00:05:01,066 --> 00:05:07,601
And in two thousand sixteen, archaeologists found a water tunnel running under the temple.

75
00:05:07,601 --> 00:05:09,634
The tomb sits over a spring.

76
00:05:09,634 --> 00:05:10,941
Keep that in mind.

77
00:05:10,941 --> 00:05:12,466
Now, we read the lid.

78
00:05:12,933 --> 00:05:14,284
The method is simple.

79
00:05:14,284 --> 00:05:20,655
We read it the way the Maya carved it, with the king at the foot of the picture, falling toward us.

80
00:05:20,655 --> 00:05:22,199
And we start at the top.

81
00:05:22,666 --> 00:05:26,388
At the top, a great bird, perched on the highest branch.

82
00:05:26,388 --> 00:05:29,645
The celestial bird, the Maya sign of the heavens.

83
00:05:29,645 --> 00:05:33,499
In the astronaut reading, this is part of a control panel.

84
00:05:33,966 --> 00:05:35,297
Below it, the tree.

85
00:05:35,297 --> 00:05:39,291
A cross-shaped world tree, the axis of the Maya universe.

86
00:05:39,291 --> 00:05:44,266
Roots in the underworld, trunk in the human world, branches in the sky.

87
00:05:44,733 --> 00:05:54,730
The same tree, with the same bird, stands on the big wall panels of the Temple of the Cross and the Temple of the Foliated Cross, in the same city.

88
00:05:54,730 --> 00:05:56,566
Nobody calls those rockets.

89
00:05:57,033 --> 00:05:58,770
In the middle, the king.

90
00:05:58,770 --> 00:06:05,287
He reclines, his body bent, dressed as the Maize God, with a turtle ornament on his chest.

91
00:06:05,287 --> 00:06:08,038
The Maize God dies, and is born again.

92
00:06:08,038 --> 00:06:09,269
So does the crop.

93
00:06:09,269 --> 00:06:10,499
So does the king.

94
00:06:10,966 --> 00:06:13,984
Beneath him, the head of a skeletal creature.

95
00:06:13,984 --> 00:06:16,129
In one reading, the Sun Monster.

96
00:06:16,129 --> 00:06:22,566
Pakal rests on it, halfway between the underworld and the living world, at the moment of sunset.

97
00:06:23,033 --> 00:06:25,022
And around him, open jaws.

98
00:06:25,022 --> 00:06:30,301
The jaws of the earth, the doorway into Xibalba, the Maya underworld.

99
00:06:30,301 --> 00:06:34,433
The astronaut reading calls those jaws exhaust flames.

100
00:06:34,899 --> 00:06:39,107
The border is a band of cosmic signs: sun, moon, star.

101
00:06:39,107 --> 00:06:42,380
Along it, the heads of six named noblemen.

102
00:06:42,380 --> 00:06:45,419
A sky, seen from above, with witnesses.

103
00:06:45,419 --> 00:06:46,666
Not a dashboard.

104
00:06:47,133 --> 00:06:52,555
The sides of the sarcophagus show Pakal's ancestors, emerging from the earth.

105
00:06:52,555 --> 00:06:54,737
A family tree, quite literally.

106
00:06:54,737 --> 00:06:57,413
This is not the portrait of a visitor.

107
00:06:57,413 --> 00:06:59,666
It is the portrait of a dynasty.

108
00:07:00,133 --> 00:07:03,059
Scholars still argue about the posture.

109
00:07:03,059 --> 00:07:08,762
Linda Schele saw Pakal falling down the Milky Way into the southern horizon.

110
00:07:08,762 --> 00:07:11,989
Others see him rising, reborn, up the tree.

111
00:07:11,989 --> 00:07:16,566
Falling or rising, the story is the same: death, then return.

112
00:07:17,033 --> 00:07:18,049
The experiment.

113
00:07:18,049 --> 00:07:22,522
We take the astronaut claim seriously, and check every part of it.

114
00:07:22,522 --> 00:07:24,013
Part one: orientation.

115
00:07:24,013 --> 00:07:26,656
The slab lay flat over the king's body.

116
00:07:26,656 --> 00:07:32,348
The carving is meant to be read with Pakal falling toward the bottom of the picture.

117
00:07:32,348 --> 00:07:35,533
To get a seat, you must turn it ninety degrees.

118
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:37,675
Part two: the controls.

119
00:07:37,675 --> 00:07:40,661
In a real capsule, every dial does a job.

120
00:07:40,661 --> 00:07:47,727
The astronaut reading gives the tree, the bird, and the monster head the job of dials and levers.

121
00:07:47,727 --> 00:07:51,733
It never explains how a stone relief controls anything.

122
00:07:52,199 --> 00:07:53,773
Part three: the flames.

123
00:07:53,773 --> 00:07:56,373
A rocket's exhaust is a plume of fire.

124
00:07:56,373 --> 00:08:06,566
The jaws at the bottom of the lid have teeth, a lower jaw, and a standard shape used again and again in Maya art for the entrance to the other world.

125
00:08:07,033 --> 00:08:08,473
Part four: the mask.

126
00:08:08,473 --> 00:08:12,289
One version of the claim reads a breathing apparatus.

127
00:08:12,289 --> 00:08:16,321
Maya burials place a jade bead in the mouth of the dead.

128
00:08:16,321 --> 00:08:17,617
Jade meant breath.

129
00:08:17,617 --> 00:08:19,633
The tomb contained the jade.

130
00:08:20,100 --> 00:08:21,296
Part five: names.

131
00:08:21,296 --> 00:08:29,605
The texts carved on the sarcophagus record who he was, when he was born, when he came to the throne, and when he died.

132
00:08:29,605 --> 00:08:33,900
The alien has a Maya name, a Maya mother, and a Maya funeral.

133
00:08:34,366 --> 00:08:36,704
Part six: the rest of the grave.

134
00:08:36,704 --> 00:08:41,671
If he came from the stars, we would expect something from the stars.

135
00:08:41,671 --> 00:08:46,565
We find cinnabar, jade, shell, obsidian, and nine stucco guardians.

136
00:08:46,565 --> 00:08:49,633
Classic Maya royal burial, piece by piece.

137
00:08:50,100 --> 00:08:50,899
The result.

138
00:08:50,899 --> 00:08:56,861
The rotated image needs a new seat, new controls, new flames, and a new passenger.

139
00:08:56,861 --> 00:08:59,623
The unrotated image needs nothing new.

140
00:08:59,623 --> 00:09:06,966
Penn Museum's Stephen Epstein recommends the tool: never postulate complexity unless it is necessary.

141
00:09:07,433 --> 00:09:12,546
But there is a real mystery in this tomb, and it has nothing to do with aliens.

142
00:09:12,546 --> 00:09:15,200
The bones did not seem to match the king.

143
00:09:15,666 --> 00:09:21,479
The inscriptions say Pakal died at eighty, after sixty-eight years on the throne.

144
00:09:21,479 --> 00:09:28,799
Ruz's anthropologists examined the bones at the site and judged the man to be between forty and fifty.

145
00:09:29,266 --> 00:09:30,799
Ruz took it seriously.

146
00:09:30,799 --> 00:09:37,700
He suggested the texts might describe two men with the same name, or count time in a different way.

147
00:09:38,166 --> 00:09:39,992
The epigraphers disagreed.

148
00:09:39,992 --> 00:09:46,594
Linda Schele and Peter Mathews, reading the birth and death glyphs, said the dates were clear.

149
00:09:46,594 --> 00:09:47,718
A man of eighty.

150
00:09:47,718 --> 00:09:51,299
Everything we know of the Maya calendar backs them.

151
00:09:51,766 --> 00:09:53,522
Later analysis settled it.

152
00:09:53,522 --> 00:10:01,356
The man in the tomb could not have lived fewer than fifty years, and most likely died in his eighth or ninth decade.

153
00:10:01,356 --> 00:10:03,112
The problem was the teeth.

154
00:10:03,112 --> 00:10:04,733
They showed little wear.

155
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:08,327
The probable reason: a king eats soft food.

156
00:10:08,327 --> 00:10:12,981
Without the gritty diet of the commoners, the teeth wear slowly.

157
00:10:12,981 --> 00:10:15,600
His heavy tartar fits that diet too.

158
00:10:16,066 --> 00:10:19,616
A real scientific dispute, solved with evidence.

159
00:10:19,616 --> 00:10:22,204
That is how a real mystery behaves.

160
00:10:22,204 --> 00:10:24,940
It gets smaller when you look closer.

161
00:10:24,940 --> 00:10:27,233
The astronaut only gets bigger.

162
00:10:27,700 --> 00:10:30,113
So why does the astronaut survive?

163
00:10:30,113 --> 00:10:37,566
Stephen Epstein, in Penn Museum's Expedition magazine, named it the looks-like-a-spaceman-to-me argument.

164
00:10:38,033 --> 00:10:42,440
His example: a child says, that cloud looks like a doggie.

165
00:10:42,440 --> 00:10:45,859
Has the child told you anything about clouds?

166
00:10:45,859 --> 00:10:46,695
About dogs?

167
00:10:46,695 --> 00:10:48,366
Or only about himself?

168
00:10:48,833 --> 00:10:53,359
The lid looks like a pilot to us because our century has pilots.

169
00:10:53,359 --> 00:10:58,097
Nobody in Palenque in the year six eighty-three had seen a cockpit.

170
00:10:58,097 --> 00:11:01,633
They had seen kings, trees, birds, and the sunset.

171
00:11:02,100 --> 00:11:04,115
Epstein names the deeper cost.

172
00:11:04,115 --> 00:11:08,414
In this story the ancient builders could not make their own art.

173
00:11:08,414 --> 00:11:10,294
It had to fall from the sky.

174
00:11:10,294 --> 00:11:15,399
In this view, he writes, the people who went before us were not fully human.

175
00:11:15,866 --> 00:11:19,164
And Maya writing is now largely deciphered.

176
00:11:19,164 --> 00:11:24,762
The sarcophagus texts record his accession, his death, and his ancestors.

177
00:11:24,762 --> 00:11:26,833
In Maya, on the same stone.

178
00:11:27,299 --> 00:11:29,243
Today the legend lives on.

179
00:11:29,243 --> 00:11:32,159
Still on documentaries, mugs and memes.

180
00:11:32,159 --> 00:11:37,766
Often with the picture turned sideways, exactly as in nineteen sixty-eight.

181
00:11:38,233 --> 00:11:41,554
A debunked reading does not make the lid boring.

182
00:11:41,554 --> 00:11:49,510
It shows a king turning his own death into a story about the sun, the maize, the Milky Way, and the return of life.

183
00:11:49,510 --> 00:11:52,900
Of the two stories, the Maya one is the stranger.

184
00:11:53,366 --> 00:11:55,014
Time to count the evidence.

185
00:11:55,014 --> 00:11:58,433
Back up the stair, to the surface, and into the debrief.

186
00:11:58,899 --> 00:11:59,447
Debrief.

187
00:11:59,447 --> 00:12:01,566
Time to score the lid of Pakal.

188
00:12:02,033 --> 00:12:07,315
A Maya king, named in the texts, buried with Maya kingship goods: evidence.

189
00:12:07,315 --> 00:12:14,498
The world tree, the bird, the jaws, the Maize God dress, all repeated elsewhere in Palenque: evidence.

190
00:12:14,498 --> 00:12:20,766
The astronaut reading needs the picture turned ninety degrees: that is its own admission.

191
00:12:21,233 --> 00:12:26,546
Pakal falling or rising: still debated, both inside Maya belief.

192
00:12:26,546 --> 00:12:28,621
The age mismatch: solved.

193
00:12:28,621 --> 00:12:31,858
A rocket, a seat, a pilot: no evidence.

194
00:12:31,858 --> 00:12:34,100
Alien visitor: no evidence.

195
00:12:34,566 --> 00:12:35,351
The verdict.

196
00:12:35,351 --> 00:12:36,463
Not an astronaut.

197
00:12:36,463 --> 00:12:41,695
A king at the foot of the world tree, falling and rising through the underworld.

198
00:12:41,695 --> 00:12:50,066
The real wonder is a hidden stair, a sealed vault, and a stone slab that tells the whole story, if you turn it the right way up.

199
00:12:50,533 --> 00:12:55,564
Thirteen centuries ago, a family carved a goodbye to a king they loved.

200
00:12:55,564 --> 00:12:58,752
The stranger in the story was never an alien.

201
00:12:58,752 --> 00:13:02,366
It was us, looking at the wrong end of the picture.

202
00:13:02,833 --> 00:13:09,647
Next case: a towering painted figure on a Saharan cliff, with a round head and no face.

203
00:13:09,647 --> 00:13:11,448
Rock art, or a visitor?

204
00:13:11,448 --> 00:13:14,033
The Great Martian God of Tassili.

205
00:13:14,500 --> 00:13:17,448
What do you see in the lid, a pilot or a king?

206
00:13:17,448 --> 00:13:22,833
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
